# IntentTarget Pro - Core Project Rules

## Tech Stack & Environment
- Platform: WordPress 6.x Core / WooCommerce
- Language Standards: Object-Oriented PHP, JavaScript, Clean HTML5/CSS

## Naming & White-Labelling Conventions
- ALL newly created or modified custom functions must strictly use the prefix 'lee_dev_' followed by a descriptive phrase and a random 4-digit unique integer suffix.
- Example: function lee_dev_dynamic_categories_loop_4912()

## Localization & Grammar Rules
- Enforce strict UK English text parameters across all strings and labels.
- Mandated spelling variants: 'licence' (noun), 'licise'/'license' (verb actions), 'customise', 'optimise', 'authorised', 'unauthorised'.

## Memory & Performance Guardrails
- Core keyword loops must run inside background chunked batch cron sequences ('cit_hourly_tracking_batch_event').
- Limit raw processing blocks to a maximum of 50 assets per processing cycle to safeguard host server RAM.

## Add ON development Rules
- Never create separate plugins or separate .zip files for new features.
- All add-on code (e.g., Webhooks, Quizzes, AI) must be built directly into the core intenttargetpro codebase.
- Feature Gating: Every new add-on must be wrapped in the lee_dev_is_addon_active_3812( 'addon-slug' ) helper method. If the user has not purchased the add-on (verified via the Master Hub API payload saved in wp_options), the code must remain completely dormant.
- Visibility: The UI/settings for the add-on should remain visible in the admin dashboard but be disabled, showing a "Purchase Add-On" up-sell button if the gate returns false.